The beta particle is a positron, which is the antiparticle of an electron. When a proton decays to a neutron, it loses a positron and therefore a mass of 0.000549amu. The kinetic energy of the beta particle is therefore equal to the energy of the decay, or $E_{\beta} = 1.19MeV = 1.19 \cdot 10^{6}eV$. Since $1eV = 1.602 \cdot 10^{-19}J$, the energy of the beta particle is equal to $1.19 \cdot 10^{6} \cdot 1.602 \cdot 10^{-19} J = 1.90 \cdot 10^{-13}J$. The kinetic energy of a particle is equal to the kinetic energy of the particle divided by the mass of the particle, so we can write $E_{\beta} = \frac{1}{m} \Delta m$. Therefore, the mass of the beta particle is equal to $\frac{1}{1.90 \cdot 10^{-13}J} \cdot 1.90 \cdot 10^{-13}J = 0.001008amu$. The mass of the proton is 1.00728 amu, so the mass of the neutron is equal to $1.00728 + 0.001008 = 1.00828 amu$. The mass of the nucleus is therefore equal to $12.007 + 1.00828 = 13.01528 amu$. Rounding this to three significant figures, we have $13.015 amu$. The closest answer is $13.015 amu$ which is (C).
